# Test: one commit changes an interface address and a static route, and the
#       address ordering survives the second root joining the transaction.
#
# zmix0 is renumbered from 10.92.0.1/24 to 10.93.0.1/24 and the static section
# is rewritten in the same reload. The iface component decomposes its root; the
# static plugin registers no decomposer, so the orchestrator represents it with
# one coarse node. The graph orders every address removal before every address
# addition, and the solver places the coarse node after the additions, so the
# sequence is destroy-address, create-address, static section
# (docs/architecture/config/apply-ordering.md, "The five phases").
#
# Until 2026-09-11 the sequence was create-address, static section,
# destroy-address, which was make-before-break. The route is what makes the
# ordering load-bearing either way, and that has not changed.
#
# The static route is what makes that sequence load-bearing rather than
# cosmetic: its gateway 10.93.0.2 lies in the NEW prefix, so the kernel answers
# ENETUNREACH to a route installed before the address exists.
#
# VALIDATES: AC-1 -- the old address is removed before the new one arrives, the
#            new address is present before the route that binds it is
#            installed, and no log line says the transaction used the section
#            apply.
# PREVENTS: D1. TxCoordinator.Execute took the operation path only while no
#           participant was uncovered, so one root with a diff and no
#           decomposer dropped the WHOLE transaction to runApply, which emits
#           every participant's section apply at once and waits. The address
#           operations that were ordered correctly lost their order with it,
#           and the transaction still reported success.
#
# The order is read from the kernel's own netlink notifications
# (configApplyOrderingMixedRootDriver,
# internal/test/fixture/register_config_apply_ordering.go), because nothing on
# the apply path logs one line per operation and the final state carries no
# order at all.
#
# DISCRIMINATION: two reverts, one for each half of the order this file reads,
# both WALKED in QEMU on 2026-09-11 against the guest's own kernel. The log is
# tmp/session/2026-09-08-cbc36cee-41ac-4afd-8b71-1bae841964d9/scratch/dw/walk-1.log,
# and every daemon in it was built in one window so the reds and the green are a
# pair.
#
#   1. Unregister the constraint rule iface-remove-address-before-add-address
#      (internal/component/iface/operation.go), so the planner emits its
#      addition first. Observed: "the new address 10.93.0.1/24 arrived before
#      the old one 10.92.0.1/24 was removed".
#   2. Make sectionNodePosition (internal/component/config/transaction/solver.go)
#      answer 0, so the coarse node runs at the head instead of after the
#      addresses this commit adds. The static section then applies before its
#      gateway's prefix exists, the kernel answers ENETUNREACH, and the route
#      never lands. Observed: "the renumber and the static route did not both
#      land", over an event stream that carries the renumber and no route.
#
# The green daemon passed every step of this file.
#
# The rejected pattern is a test that asserts only the end state: the renumber
# and the route are both present whichever order they landed in, so the end
# state cannot tell the two paths apart.

# needs-linux: a dummy interface, an address, a kernel FIB route and
# `ip monitor` are kernel surfaces, and all of them need CAP_NET_ADMIN.
option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin

# Initial config: the old address, and a static route that binds it.
stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
interface {
	dummy zmix0 {
		unit 0 {
			ipv4 {
				address [ 10.92.0.1/24 ]
			}
		}
	}
}

static {
	table default {
		route 172.31.0.0/24 {
			next {
				hop 10.92.0.2 { }
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

# Reloaded config: both roots diff. The address moves prefix and the static
# section replaces its route with one whose gateway only resolves against the
# new address.
tmpfs=config2.conf:terminator=EOF_CONF2
interface {
	dummy zmix0 {
		unit 0 {
			ipv4 {
				address [ 10.93.0.1/24 ]
			}
		}
	}
}

static {
	table default {
		route 172.30.0.0/24 {
			next {
				hop 10.93.0.2 { }
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF2

option=env:var=ze.log.interface:value=info
option=env:var=ze.log.static:value=info

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze-test fixture reload/config-apply-ordering-mixed-root-driver:timeout=40s

expect=exit:code=0
expect=stderr:contains=sighup reload complete
expect=stderr:contains=OK: address ordering held across two roots
expect=stderr:contains=static routes loaded
expect=file:path=mixed-events.txt:exists=true
# This reject cannot fire, and it is kept as a tripwire rather than as
# evidence. Commit a6ea1ad0b deleted the branch that logged it, so no build
# of the product emits the string at all, and even then the line is
# logger().Info on subsystem config.transaction, which this test does not
# raise to info. Neither revert above reinstates that branch. What
# discriminates here is the positive ordering assertion above. To make this
# line fence anything, add
# option=env:var=ze.log.config.transaction:value=info and re-run the walk.
reject=stderr:contains=using section apply
reject=stderr:contains=operation dependency cycle
reject=stderr:contains=config apply partial failure
reject=stderr:contains=config verify failed
